      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the technical aspects of the oxidation and reduction processes in clay firing, which are indeed crucial for understanding the firing techniques used in the Peloponnese during the late Bronze Age.\n\nOxidation firing, also known as air-firing, occurs in an environment with plenty of oxygen. This process typically results in a brighter, more vibrant color on the pottery due to the presence of oxygen, which allows the iron compounds in the clay to oxidize and produce a range of colors from reds to yellows to oranges. The surface of the pottery will often have a smooth, glossy finish, and the colors will be more uniform.\n\nReduction firing, on the other hand, takes place in a low-oxygen environment. This process can lead to a variety of colors, including blues, greens, and blacks, depending on the specific materials used in the clay and the temperature reached during firing. The surface of the pottery may have a more matte finish, and the colors can be more varied and less uniform than in oxidation firing.\n\nWhen examining the cross-section of ceramic shards, look for signs of these processes. In oxidation firing, you might see a more uniform color and a smoother surface, while in reduction firing, the colors",
